================================================================================
WHAT APACHE-2.0 COVERS, AND WHAT IT DOES NOT
================================================================================

Apache-2.0 covers the SOURCE CODE in this repository: backend/, frontend/,
matlab/, scripts/, docs/ and the demo images in samples/ (each of which carries
its own CC0 / CC BY / public-domain provenance in samples/README.md).

It does NOT cover two things that are derived from third-party datasets:

1. THE TRAINED CHECKPOINTS (backend/weights/*.weights.h5, distributed as
   release assets, and the SavedModel exports under models/export/).
2. THE DATASET MANIFESTS (backend/data/manifests/*.csv), which list image
   identifiers, reader grades, patient groupings and perceptual hashes taken
   from the datasets below. They contain no images.

Both are made available for NON-COMMERCIAL RESEARCH AND EDUCATIONAL USE ONLY,
because they are derived from data whose terms carry that restriction. Anyone
intending commercial use must obtain their own rights from the dataset owners
and retrain. This is a restriction we inherit and pass on; it is not one we
chose, and it is not a restriction on the code.

No fundus images from any of these datasets are redistributed in this
repository or in its release assets.

================================================================================
DATASETS USED, AND THEIR TERMS
================================================================================

APTOS 2019 Blindness Detection (Asia Pacific Tele-Ophthalmology Society, via
Kaggle) — used for training.
    Kaggle competition data: competition use and non-commercial & academic
    research. NON-COMMERCIAL.
    https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/aptos2019-blindness-detection

EyePACS / Diabetic Retinopathy Detection (EyePACS LLC and the California
Healthcare Foundation, via Kaggle, 2015) — used for training, for the
patient-disjoint calibration set that the locked threshold was chosen on, and
for the secondary held-out set.
    Kaggle competition data, provided for the competition and for non-commercial
    academic research. NON-COMMERCIAL.
    https://www.kaggle.com/competitions/diabetic-retinopathy-detection

DDR (Li, Gao, Wang, Guo, Liu, Kang; Information Sciences 501:511-522, 2019) —
used for the primary external test set and for lesion segmentation.
    MIT License (see https://github.com/nkicsl/DDR-dataset). PERMISSIVE: this
    dataset alone imposes no non-commercial restriction.

Messidor-2 (ADCIS; Decenciere et al. 2014) with the adjudicated grades of
Krause et al. 2018 — used once as an additional external test set and for the
site-calibration study.
    "Can be used, free of charge, for research and educational purposes. Copy,
    redistribution, and any unauthorized commercial use are prohibited."
    NON-COMMERCIAL, NO REDISTRIBUTION. Kindly provided by the Messidor program
    partners. Required citations:
      Decenciere et al., "Feedback on a publicly distributed database: the
      Messidor database", Image Analysis & Stereology 33(3):231-234, 2014.
      Abramoff et al., "Automated analysis of retinal images for detection of
      referable diabetic retinopathy", JAMA Ophthalmology 131(3):351-357, 2013.
    https://www.adcis.net/en/third-party/messidor2/

EyeQ (Fu et al. 2019) — image-quality labels over EyePACS images, used to train
the quality classifier. Research use; inherits the EyePACS terms above.
    https://github.com/HzFu/EyeQ

MESSIDOR fovea-centre annotations (University of Huelva; Gegundez-Arias,
Marin, Bravo, Suero, "Locating the fovea center position in digital fundus
images using thresholding and feature extraction techniques", Computerized
Medical Imaging and Graphics 37(5-6):386-393, 2013) — used only to measure the
optic-disc and fovea localisation (backend/eval/landmark_check.py). The
annotations are not redistributed: the committed result holds our per-image
errors, not their coordinates.
    https://www.uhu.es/retinopathy/

Not used by this project, despite appearing in the DR literature: IDRiD and
DRIVE. No code path reads them.

